WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.700
Many protocol parsers receive input incrementally.

2
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:07.480
A network service may read a few bytes now and more bytes later.

3
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:10.860
An event loop may return whenever data is available.

4
00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:13.360
A peer may disconnect in the middle of a message.

5
00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:18.420
A robust parser treats these as normal conditions rather than exceptional surprises.

6
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:20.540
Buffers need explicit accounting.

7
00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.120
Capacity is how much storage exists.

8
00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:26.420
Used length is how many bytes currently hold input.

9
00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.040
Consumed length is how much the parser has already processed.

10
00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:33.260
Remaining space is what can safely accept more data.

11
00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.840
Mixing these facts can lead to incorrect parsing or memory safety problems.

12
00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:41.560
Partial input should leave parser state consistent.

13
00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:47.660
If the parser needs more bytes, it should remember exactly what has been received and what is still required.

14
00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:53.280
If input is rejected, it should discard or reset state according to protocol rules.

15
00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:57.220
If the peer disconnects, partial state should be cleaned up predictably.

16
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:02.140
Overlong or incomplete messages should not cause unbounded buffering.

17
00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:08.880
The parser needs maximum message size and timeout expectations so it does not keep accumulating data forever.

18
00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:15.460
When limits are exceeded, the failure path should release resources and leave the session in a defined state.

19
00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:23.740
Binary payloads should not be treated as C strings unless the protocol explicitly defines text, termination, and encoding rules.

20
00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:30.880
A message can contain zero bytes, unterminated data, or fields that are valid binary but invalid text.

21
00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:36.500
If text is required, create a bounded, validated representation deliberately.
